For each, the compliance function advises on the design and the head of the department concerned decides it.

**Position.**
- The role exists where justified by the size and nature of the business.
- The holder is a member of management.
- The board appoints the holder on the CEO's proposal.
- Some frameworks describe the SAE as first line.

**Tasks (ultimately responsible for):**
- the BWRA process: the BWRA is prepared and updated at least annually, assessed against the risk appetite, and presented by the SAE to the body that adopts it (the CEO or the board);
- internal routines and guidelines (manuals and group directives) that detail the policy and instruction, kept updated at least annually and implemented, including documented training for the first line;
- control and follow-up that the measures, routines and other procedures decided are carried out in the business;
- an appropriate organisation for the risk-based AML work, covering reporting processes, controls and risk assessments;
- checking that decisions on high-risk customers are in line with the risk appetite (four-eyes principle);
- transaction monitoring: scenario development, alert handling, investigations and escalation to the CFA;
- a decision committee for customer cases with documented rules of procedure and senior first- and second-line representation;
- establishing and owning the ML/TF models (CRR, TM, screening). The SAE is model owner unless another owner is appointed;
- a method for model risk management and validation, where the group requires one;
- periodic reporting to the supervisor `[VERIFY REFERENCE]`;
- internal reporting on investigations and measures relating to financial sanctions;
- instructing local SAEs on reporting to the group SAE, including timeframes and reporting points;

- implementing the risk appetite: detect, contain and off-board prohibited activity; a watch list of offboarded customers; restricted activity reflected in CRR and KRIs; product limitations, controls and KRIs where inherent risk is high;
- a process to examine new trends, patterns and methods (financial crime intelligence), feeding the BWRA and the scenarios;
- verifying that new or materially changed products, services, markets, processes and IT systems comply. The SAE has the right and the duty to take part in new product approval.

**Reporting to the board and CEO.** At least half-yearly in one framework and quarterly in another. Set it per client. Mandatory points:
- updates from the AML/CTF organisation, including the execution and outcome of controls;
- KRIs and KPIs (customer base, risk exposure, investigations, reporting) linked to the risk appetite;
- information and analysis of prohibited activities (limits) set by the board;
- remediation measures and the strengthening of controls to keep residual risk within the appetite;
- how the decided measures and routines are implemented;
- horizon scanning: upcoming external and internal rules;
- direct and prompt reporting of serious deficiencies and of measures taken on true sanctions hits.

**Powers.**
- Veto whenever the SAE assesses the ML/TF risk as unacceptable in relation to the risk appetite.
- May delegate tasks while remaining ultimately responsible.

**Local SAE (subsidiary or branch).**
- Responsible for the local BWRA, implementing group rules with adjustments for stricter local law, and local monitoring.
- Reports functionally to the group SAE and administratively to the local board and CEO, including appointments to and departures from AML positions.

**Position.**
- Appointed by the board, or by the CEO in some frameworks.
- Often the head of compliance (second line).
- Organisationally separate from, and independent of, the functions and areas it monitors and controls.

**Tasks:**
- ensure that reports to the FIU are made and act as the contact person for the FIU (execution may be delegated in writing);
- ensure that information is provided to the police on request, in the manner the police prescribe `[VERIFY REFERENCE]`;
- monitor and continuously control compliance with the AML/CTF law, the supervisor's regulations and the internal rules;
- monitor and control compliance with financial sanctions, including reporting to the supervisor and the FIU, and give recommendations when sanctions cases are escalated and the action to take is unclear;
- advise and support employees, contractors and others taking part in the business;
- inform and train the persons concerned, including the board and management;
- assess regularly whether the routines and guidelines are adequate and effective;
- control and assess the reasonableness of the BWRA outcome against the risk appetite;
- take part in TM scenario design from a control perspective;
- validate ML/TF models independently before they are used, and ensure validation in other group entities;
- test the AML processes independently;
- protect the identity of internal reporters and keep investigation information confidential;
- promptly report activity outside the group risk appetite and recommend that it stop, unless there is a satisfactory remediation plan;
- set reporting requirements for the SAE and the business, and standards for SAR quality;
- instruct local CFAs on reporting to the group CFA;
- cooperate with the other control functions.

**Annual risk-based plan:**
- control activities: sample controls, reviews of specific areas, monitoring of KRIs and KPIs;

- training of employees and the board;
- activities to ensure that the routines are adequate and efficient.

The plans of significant subsidiaries are part of the group plan.

**Reporting to the board and CEO.** At least half-yearly, or quarterly in some frameworks; it may form part of the compliance report. Mandatory points:
- a summary of the risk exposure;
- horizon scanning;
- the controls and training carried out;
- the annual plan and an evaluation of resources;
- contacts with authorities;
- compliance risks and the KRIs and KPIs for FIU reporting;
- direct reporting of matters of principle or material matters.

**Powers.**
- Veto when the ML/TF risk is unacceptable in relation to the risk appetite.
- Veto, within the onboarding process, over very high-risk customers, restricted customers and PEP/RCA customers.
- The group CFA is consulted before changes to local CFAs and has a veto over such changes and over cuts in their resources.
- Staff responsibility for the function: recruitment, work management, appraisals, salary, competence and working environment.

**Independence criteria** (from a delivered policy for a non-financial obliged entity):
- the CFA performs no tasks within the business it monitors;
- the CFA is organisationally separate from the functions it monitors;
- the CFA's remuneration is not designed in a way that endangers objectivity.

- **Onboarding and KYC:** onboarding customers, including the risk assessment; periodic and event-driven updates of CDD.
- **Investigations:**
  - investigating alerts from TM and from internal reports;
  - assessing whether to report to the FIU, where that decision is delegated;
  - investigating sanctions screening hits;
  - acting on true hits: freezing funds, blocking engagements, and reporting internally and to the supervisor and the FIU.
- **Controls:** control activities to ensure that the most significant mitigating measures work.
- **Advice and support:** supporting the business areas day to day; taking part in the high-risk forum; planning and running AML training.
- **Processes and rules:** driving process development and maintaining the AML instructions and routines; preparing material for branches and coordinating communication.
- **Powers:** decisions with a major impact are taken in consultation with the head of AML or the SAE.

- **Business area heads:**
  - ultimately responsible for implementing the group's processes in their area and for the ML/TF risk in their legal entity;
  - decide in line with the risk appetite;
  - ensure that CDD and monitoring follow the instruction and the manuals;
  - take part in the BWRA;
  - may delegate execution while keeping the responsibility.
- **Management team (financial crime scope):**
  - sufficient KYC actions;
  - new product approval and change processes that consider financial crime risk;
  - staff education;
  - escalation to the MLRO and the SAE;
  - outsourced functions handled under the client's instructions;
  - ABC procedures at every level.
- **Branch and department managers:**
  - staff know and apply the rules and complete training;
  - direct contact with customers when CDD questions arise;
  - practical support to staff;
  - reporting suspicions without delay to the AML function, and deviations to compliance.
- **All employees:**
  - prevent, report to their manager and the AML function, take part in training, follow the rules and perform CDD;
  - personal criminal liability for intentional or grossly negligent participation, for neglecting the duty to scrutinise or report, and for breaching confidentiality.

- **Model owner:** responsible for the whole model lifecycle except validation. This covers internal guidelines, development and data quality, maintenance, documentation, implementation, deployment and risk tiering. It also covers annual review and reporting, identifying the need for new models, including models in continuity planning, remediating validation findings and communicating with stakeholders.
- **SAE:** ultimately responsible for the AML models and their risk management; approves minor changes; requests validation of significant changes.
- **CFA:** controls model risk; must be informed of manual overrides and of risks deliberately not covered; is responsible for independent validation and may engage internal or external parties.
- **Model forum:**
  - keeps the inventory;
  - sets up the validation team;
  - reviews validation reports before the CFA signs off;
  - recommends use, redevelopment or retirement.
- **Validation team:** independent of the development of the model. It combines data management, statistical and AML regulatory competence.
